Title: "Matrices and Matrix Algebra"
Id: "9b0cf886-b3ca-47a3-a190-d69788b15044"


Text: "A *matrix* is a two-dimensional rectangular grid of scalars arranged into
       rows and columns. Conceived in the nineteenth century by Arthur Cayley
       and James Joseph Sylvester, matrices provide the computational machinery
       that translates abstract, coordinate-free linear algebra into concrete
       numerical algorithms.

       When finite-dimensional vector spaces are equipped with ordered bases,
       every linear transformation is faithfully encoded by a matrix whose
       columns are the coordinate vectors of the basis images. In this
       representation, function composition transforms into matrix
       multiplication, vector evaluation corresponds to matrix-vector products,
       and geometric transformations such as rotations, reflections, and
       projections become structured array computations."
Id: "f8831faa-ad1b-46ed-838f-5c92d3cf41b1"


SectionTitle: "Matrix Definitions"
Id: "030a4360-39bf-4fb0-844f-5c5644f15392"


[\matrix:of.dim{m, n}:over{F}]
Declares: A ::= a(i_, j_)
when:
. m, n is \\anything
. F is \field
Documented:
. called: "matrix of dimension $m? \times n?$ over $F?$"
. writing: a(i, j)
  as: "a?_{i?, j?}"
. writing: a(i_, j_)
  as: "\left[ a?_{i_?, j_?} \right]"
. description: "A matrix $A = [a_{i,j}]$ is a rectangular array of scalars
                indexed by row and column."
Id: "9cfabd0d-3e74-46fa-989b-b8a7230a61f3"


[\identity.matrix:of.dim{n}:over{F}]
Declares: I ::= delta(i_, j_) is \matrix:of.dim{n, n}:over{F}
when:
. n is \\anything
. F is \field
satisfies:
. forAll: i, j is \natural
  then:
  . piecewise:
    if: i = j
    then: delta(i, j) = 1
    else: delta(i, j) = 0
Documented:
. called: "identity matrix of dimension $n?$ over $F?$"
. written: "I_{n?}"
. description: "The $n \times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ with ones on the main
                diagonal and zeros elsewhere."
Id: "1e35b9e5-6fba-4b93-8a90-50ae1b86687a"


SectionTitle: "Matrix Operations"
Id: "998a9c55-04b9-48eb-89f3-cbe064c230cf"


[A(i_, j_) \.matrix.+./ B(i_, j_)]
Defines: C(i_, j_) is \matrix:of.dim{m, n}:over{F}
using:
. m, n is \\anything
. F ::= (X, +, *, 0, 1) is \field
when: A, B is \matrix:of.dim{m, n}:over{F}
expresses:
. forAll: i, j is \natural
  then: C(i, j) = A(i, j) + B(i, j)
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ plus $B?$"
. written: "A? + B?"
. description: "Componentwise addition of matrices of identical dimension:
                $(A + B)_{i,j} = A_{i,j} + B_{i,j}$."
Id: "dd197d03-0f2e-4003-8330-a8c3e8e09dd6"


[A(i_, k_) \.matrix.*./ B(k_, j_)]
Defines: C(i_, j_) is \matrix:of.dim{m, p}:over{F}
using:
. m, n, p is \\anything
. F is \field
when:
. A is \matrix:of.dim{m, n}:over{F}
. B is \matrix:of.dim{n, p}:over{F}
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ times $B?$"
. written: "A? B?"
. description: "Matrix multiplication $C = AB$, where
                $C_{i,j} = \sum_k A_{i,k} B_{k,j}$."
Id: "ab389a4a-5187-4af5-b2f3-c62e0c3b08db"


[\matrix.transpose:of{A(i_, j_)}]
Defines: At(j_, i_) is \matrix:of.dim{n, m}:over{F}
using:
. m, n is \\anything
. F is \field
when: A is \matrix:of.dim{m, n}:over{F}
expresses:
. forAll: i, j is \natural
  then: At(j, i) = A(i, j)
Documented:
. called: "transpose of $A?$"
. written: "A?^{T}"
. description: "The transpose $A^T$ obtained by reflecting $A$ across its main
                diagonal: $(A^T)_{j,i} = A_{i,j}$."
Id: "82ba3c4b-40a8-4dfc-8adc-69eabb25f1ec"


SectionTitle: "Determinants, Trace, and Eigenvalues"
Id: "d2d9444b-7931-4501-80cf-6a9dcb0337ef"


[\determinant:of{A(i_, j_)}]
Defines: d "in" F
using:
. n is \\anything
. F is \field
when: A is \matrix:of.dim{n, n}:over{F}
Documented:
. called: "determinant of $A?$"
. written: "\det(A?)"
. description: "The determinant $\det(A)$ is the unique alternating multilinear
                function of the columns mapping $I_n$ to $1$."
Id: "0552914d-9a49-4089-88ba-72916ea96b3e"


[\matrix.trace:of{A(i_, j_)}]
Defines: tr "in" F
using:
. n is \\anything
. F is \field
when: A is \matrix:of.dim{n, n}:over{F}
Documented:
. called: "trace of $A?$"
. written: "\operatorname{tr}(A?)"
. description: "The trace $\operatorname{tr}(A) = \sum_{i=1}^n A_{i,i}$ is the
                sum of the diagonal elements."
Id: "88fa45dd-3b56-49ae-8617-08fbab297577"


[\eigenvalue:of{A(i_, j_)}]
Declares: lambda "in" F
using:
. n is \\anything
. F is \field
when: A is \matrix:of.dim{n, n}:over{F}
satisfies:
. exists: v is \matrix:of.dim{n, 1}:over{F}
  suchThat: v != \identity.matrix:of.dim{n}:over{F}
Documented:
. called: "eigenvalue of $A?$"
. written: "\lambda"
. description: "A scalar $\lambda \in F$ such that $Av = \lambda v$ for some
                nonzero column vector $v$."
Id: "6ed950a4-63a3-437a-871c-23c49564425a"


TextTheorem: "Let $V$ and $W$ be finite-dimensional vector spaces with ordered bases
              $\mathcal{B} = \{v_1, \dots, v_n\}$ and $\mathcal{C} = \{w_1, \dots, w_m\}$.
              The coordinate representation mapping:
              $$ \mathcal{M}_{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{C}} : \mathcal{L}(V, W) \longrightarrow M_{m \times n}(F), \quad T \longmapsto [T]_{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{C}} $$
              is an isomorphism of vector spaces.
              
              Furthermore, if $U$ is a third space with basis $\mathcal{D}$ and $S \in \mathcal{L}(W, U)$,
              then the matrix of the composite transformation satisfies:
              $$ [S \circ T]_{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{D}} = [S]_{\mathcal{C}}^{\mathcal{D}} \cdot [T]_{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{C}}. $$
              The classical row-by-column matrix multiplication rule $(AB)_{i,j} = \sum_k A_{i,k} B_{k,j}$
              is not an arbitrary convention; it is the unique algebraic formula forced
              by the composition of linear maps."
Id: "c5d30a1a-6c29-4f04-b1f0-e16fe60d6449"


TextDefinition: "The *determinant* $\det : M_{n \times n}(F) \to F$ is the unique
                 multilinear, alternating function of the column vectors that assigns
                 the value $1$ to the identity matrix $I_n$.
                 
                 It is given explicitly by the Leibniz formula:
                 $$ \det(A) = \sum_{\sigma \in S_n} \operatorname{sgn}(\sigma) \prod_{i=1}^n A_{\sigma(i), i}. $$
                 
                 The determinant possesses fundamental algebraic properties:
                 1. Multiplicativity: $\det(AB) = \det(A) \det(B)$.
                 2. Invertibility: A square matrix $A$ is invertible in $M_{n \times n}(F)$
                    if and only if $\det(A) \neq 0$.
                 3. Geometric volume: Over $\mathbb{R}$, $|\det(A)|$ measures the volume
                    scaling factor of the parallelotope spanned by the column vectors.
                 4. Similarity invariance: $\det(P^{-1}AP) = \det(A)$, proving that the
                    determinant is an intrinsic invariant of a linear operator $T$,
                    independent of the choice of basis."
Id: "004b4c6d-979c-42f9-95bf-c154d662a6f9"


TextDefinition: "For a linear endomorphism $T \in \mathcal{L}(V)$ on an
                 $n$-dimensional vector space, the *characteristic polynomial*
                 is: $$ \chi_T(t) = \det(tI - T). $$ A scalar $\lambda \in F$ is
                 an *eigenvalue* of $T$ if and only if $\chi_T(\lambda) = 0$,
                 which occurs if and only if the operator $T - \lambda I$ has a
                 nontrivial kernel.

                 The corresponding non-zero vectors $v \in \ker(T - \lambda I)$
                 are *eigenvectors*, satisfying $T(v) = \lambda v$. The subspace
                 $E_\lambda = \ker(T - \lambda I)$ is the *eigenspace* of
                 $\lambda$. The operator $T$ is diagonalizable if and only if
                 $V$ possesses a basis consisting entirely of eigenvectors."
Id: "2fd95aa6-aab1-4a5f-bc72-31073514ac0d"


TextTheorem: "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem. Every square matrix $A \in M_{n \times n}(F)$
              satisfies its own characteristic polynomial: $\chi_A(A) = 0$.
              
              *Proof Sketch*: Consider the adjugate matrix $B(t) = \operatorname{adj}(tI - A)$.
              By the fundamental property of the classical adjugate:
              $$ (tI - A) B(t) = \det(tI - A) I_n = \chi_A(t) I_n. $$
              The entries of $B(t)$ are polynomials in $t$ of degree at most $n-1$,
              so $B(t)$ can be expanded as a matrix polynomial:
              $$ B(t) = B_{n-1} t^{n-1} + B_{n-2} t^{n-2} + \cdots + B_1 t + B_0 $$
              with coefficient matrices $B_k \in M_{n \times n}(F)$.
              If $\chi_A(t) = t^n + c_{n-1} t^{n-1} + \cdots + c_1 t + c_0$, equating
              matrix coefficients of powers of $t$ in $(tI - A) B(t) = \chi_A(t) I_n$ gives:
              $$ B_{n-1} = I, \quad B_{k-1} - A B_k = c_k I \quad (1 \leq k \leq n-1), \quad -A B_0 = c_0 I. $$
              Multiplying each equation on the left by $A^k$ and summing telescopically yields:
              $$ \chi_A(A) = A^n + c_{n-1} A^{n-1} + \cdots + c_1 A + c_0 I = 0. $$
              
              The Cayley-Hamilton theorem establishes that $A^n$ can always be expressed
              as a linear combination of lower powers $\{I, A, A^2, \dots, A^{n-1}\}$,
              providing an invaluable computational tool for matrix powers, matrix exponentials,
              and inverse computation."
Id: "516a32e9-0f71-474f-aa89-62a88dff1cb9"
